/**

\page tarch_plotting Plotting

All plotting in Peano is typically realised through the files in tarch/plotter.
An action set dumps the data. To do so, it owns one file writer, and its
observer clones write into that writer. Each clone claims one ordered
piece, and the writer combines these pieces into one snapshot per action set
and time stamp.


## Action set pattern

Plotting action sets create the file writer in `prepareTraversal()`. Each
observer clone claims its piece in `beginTraversal()` and fills that piece
during its callbacks. `unprepareTraversal()` writes the completed snapshot
after all clones have joined. The action set owns its writers through
`std::unique_ptr`.


## File writers and time series

The plotting is split into two responsibilities. The actual data of one
traversal is written through a concrete subclass of the abstract
tarch::plotter::FileWriter. Peano ships VTU (tarch::plotter::VTUFileWriter),
HDF5 (tarch::plotter::HDF5FileWriter) and NetCDF
(tarch::plotter::NetCDFFileWriter) implementations. A file writer receives an
output directory. `acquirePiece()` allocates an ordered piece, and the
`create...()` factory methods bind vertex, cell, and data plotters to it.

The bookkeeping over all snapshots, i.e. the index/meta file that ties the
individual snapshots into one time series, is written separately by a subclass
of tarch::plotter::TimeSeriesWriter. VTU uses
tarch::plotter::PVDTimeSeriesWriter, HDF5 uses
tarch::plotter::XDMFTimeSeriesWriter, and NetCDF uses
tarch::plotter::JSONTimeSeriesWriter. VTKHDF keeps its time steps in one file.

A plotting action set typically owns both writers as `std::unique_ptr`. The time
series writer is created once and kept alive for the whole simulation, while
the file writer is recreated for each snapshot with empty piece buffers:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  if (_timeSeriesWriter == nullptr) {
    _timeSeriesWriter = std::make_unique<tarch::plotter::PVDTimeSeriesWriter>();
  }

  _fileWriter.reset();
  _fileWriter = std::make_unique<tarch::plotter::VTUFileWriter>(outputPath);
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Each observer clone claims its piece with a unique ordering key before it
creates the associated data plotters:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  const int pieceId = _fileWriter->acquirePiece(observerId);
  auto cellPlotter = _fileWriter->createCellPlotter(pieceId);
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Once a traversal has filled the file writer, the snapshot is committed and
registered with the time series in one go:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  _timeSeriesWriter->writeSnapshotFile(filename, time, *_fileWriter);
  _timeSeriesWriter->writeTimeSeriesMetaFile(
    outputPath, filename
  );
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

writeSnapshotFile() delegates to the file writer to dump the actual snapshot
and remembers the (time, filename) pair, and writeTimeSeriesMetaFile() rewrites
the index/meta file so that the new snapshot becomes visible to the
post-processing tools.


## Parallel plotting

The concrete file writer owns the MPI coordination for a snapshot. VTU writes
one `.vtu` file per rank and a global `.pvtu` descriptor; collective HDF5 and
NetCDF writers agree on a field schema, including on empty ranks. Time-series
metadata is written by the global master where the format requires it.


*/
